n one patient and if  you change between different patients  the difference is probably even higher  the same applies to saliva where you  have different viscosities  whatever the patient did eat before  or what he did drink  as a nest test you need to decide  is your analyte accessible to a sandwich  assay so that would have multiple  binding sites or do you need to move to  a competitive essay which is typically a  bit more tricky to control as you really  need to control the conjugate release  here  always a critical choice is what  materials do you use in your lateral  flow essay for sample pads wick pads  conjugate pads but also for the membrane  especially for the membrane  you have to consider what the influence  of these is on your test do you use a  slow membrane  where you have a high  time for your analyte added test and  control line or do you use a fast  membrane do you need  thick or thin wicking pads to speed up  the flow and store liquid do you need to  separate blood at the start of the test  a very complex topic which we could talk  hours about  the next thing you need to  take a look at  is your reagents what are you using are  you working with antibodies are they  polyclonal or anti-air monoclonal are  you using alpha mass optimus heptines  for all of these they have all have  different  properties  however  some  criteria always apply to them they need  to be clean they need to be  reproducible  and they need to have the right binding  kinetics  if you now have your test system settled  down from the materials reagents and  analyte set you should look at  manufacturing which we will do later on  as well  what are the most critical processes  for your manufacturing  because these can affect the  [Music]  outcome of the lateral flow test  and in the end we will take a look at  various reader systems today as well  what can you do  with the readers what are pros and cons  of various readers  we'll cover that later  story short of all these tasks of  for you as a developer is  that your ultimate goal is to minimize  the uncertainty of your measurement as  far as possible because lower your  uncertainty is the more precise your  quantitation can be  if we look at the difference  for a readout between sandwich and  competitive tests  we see certain differences as i  described earlier  a sandwich test works with an increase  in signal intensity over the analyte  concentration  it is typically a sigmoidal curve  which gives you various windows of  operation you have a lower detection  limit which is a set just to be used  theoretically  but you also have your measurement area  between the lower limit and the upper  limit of quantification which is  typically a more or less linear  linear area and the flatter the linear  part of this curve is the wider is the  range of concentration that you can  cover with your test signal system  if you however move to two high analyte  concentrations you see a decrease  in the reader signal again  this is the so-called hook effect  basically you have too much analyte for  your conjugate  and you start binding  unmarked or unconjugated analyte to your  test line and therefore you have a  decrease in your reader signal so make  sure that you always work between your  lower and your upper limit of  quantification for your analyte  if we look at competitive tests we see  already that it works exactly the  opposite  we have a decrease in signal  with an increase in analyte  concentration once again a sigmoidal  curve distribution  and the steepness of the curve  um  also again determines the concentration  range that you can cover with your test  system make sure to always work between  your lower and upper limit of  quantification  and if you see an effect of  signal at very high analyte  concentrations it is typically  non-specific binding of the detector  but you can just mathematically in your  re-dot software  get rid of that  let's come to  material selection  we're talking typically or mainly about  membranes and papers but for  some tests we are also talking about  glass fibers  all of these materials are typically  squamous random structures especially  the membranes they are mainly defined by  thickness and capillary flow time  and the capillary flow time represents  how fast your test moves through the  membrane or also your test system  papers  such as cellulose papers or also the  glass fibers are mainly used for the  wick pad and conjugate as well as sample  preparation pen they are often soaked in  buffer  you do this to adjust for ph value  viscosity salt concentrations  or as mentioned to purify blood plasma  you need to optimize your buffer  composition  always to the lowest required  concentration at the thin end of the  paper specification because your paper  will always have very inner thickness  you  you typically have like a 20 range rough  for example and always optimized for the  thin end of the specification because  otherwise you will have a oversaturated  system  when you are modifying or soaking your  conjugate pads with a conjugate solution  you should never soak them for a  quantitative test the reason  for that is once again the thickness  variation in your conjugate pads  if you soak a material  you  cannot control how much corn you get you  deposit on it you should rather  dispense your conjugate pad with high  precision equipment to guarantee that  always the same  amount of conjugate is present and  therefore your test is controlled  for the membranes you typically need to  block them to avoid unspecific binding  of analytes or detectors to the membrane  metric  the good and bad thing about  nitrocellulose is that it binds proteins  but you only want to bind it at your  test and control line  therefore you can use various blocking  approaches one of the most commons  the block on the slide  is not recommended to be used for  quantitative essays  the problem with that is that the block  on the fly typically is carried out  while running the test itself but you  also get a  concentration gradient  by running your blocking agent  and this makes the test unreliable we  recommend that you use an additional  manufacturing step  for blocking the membrane in a  controlled manner  okay and one thing that you always need  to be  aware of is that the  reaction time of the reagents at the  test line is very very short  that's why you should test your membrane  for the upper and lower specification  end of the capillary flow time  you can do that by asking your material  supplier for limiting samples which are  basically at the edge of the  specification  but it might happen that you also need  to work with a customized membrane  if we look at the membranes a bit more  in detail  and especially at the capillary rise  time we see that it has a high effect on  your test itself  we typically work  a set in specification ranges  and  ideally  your reagents would be compatible with a  full membrane specification  unfortunately this is not often the case  if you use for example a membrane that  is too slow there's a high chance of  false positive signals because your  reagent maintains at  the test line  too long and you typically get very high  background sickness  for very fast membranes you have the  opposite your agent is not long enough  at the test line to bind  to to the conjugate and to the detector  and  therefore you get false negative results  so you really need to balance out the  speed of your membrane  um  in rare cases you have issues with both  specification edges  and that's where the challenge begins  and you need to talk about customization  if we look at various reagents that you  need for your tests  first you have to look at your antibody  and the  system which can also be abdominals  affirmations monoclonal polyclonal  antibodies  and it's essential that you have a  consistent quality of these it is  recommended that you test every batch  for its kinetic properties 