ertainty and therefore  not wrong signal but  not the right result in your test  and what we see in the field is that  this is really a major reason for an  increase in uncertainty  so make sure that you have your process  in control for conjugation  it  typically it also is that you need to be  providing standard curves for  your readers  with every new lot of conjugate to  remove variability  if we move further on to lamination  the lamination is  bringing all your membranes and the pads  sample pad wick pack conjugation pad  to  a  support cut you basically laminate them  on there and make sure that they are in  the right position let's say the flow  can be consistent and your material is  produced in a way  as design  however the challenge in this is the  material overlap as we are talking of  only  one or two millimeters of material  overlap  it is hard to establish that in  manufacturing environment especially as  the materials  can vary a bit they are not 100 the same  as we're talking always about  specification ranges  um for small  test numbers  many  people many companies go for manual  lamination processes however these are  not very reproducible you will get a lot  of variation in your material overlap  and therefore also a lot of uncertainty  in your quantitative test essays  therefore it is highly recommended to  use an automated lamination system  with a built-in  control and positioning  of your various test components  the dispensing of your test and control  line but also of your conjugate  is where your actual test result gets  visible so this is the the error that  you will see straight away  it is essential that you control the  dispensing  and that  you filter your dispensing solutions  prior to use because you do not want to  have any dispensing artifacts on there  make sure to control your environmental  conditions especially humidity  when dispensing onto your membrane for  example  and always align your dispensing  parameters with the thickness  variability of the membrane if you're  working with a very thick membrane  you need to  properly use more dispensing liquids  than your work with a very thin membrane  one thing that is always  to mention is  take care of your equipment  change dispensing equipment  as early as possible do preventive  maintenance  because  every error that you do here you will  see straight away in the market  the last aspect that we will handle  today is the drying process it looks  like a very simple process but  it is very challenging and very tough  you can make a lot of things right but  also a lot of things wrong here  the drying applies to  everything in your  test system it is for pads after coating  it is for trying to conjugate  for testing control lines but also for  drying the membrane after blocking  and  you should standardize your drying  processes  standardize your temperature standardize  your times and standardize your  remaining humidity  if possible use forced air convection  and do not dry  just at the air  a it takes very long to dry at superior  but you also get drying artifacts where  your sample does not dry homogeneously  which is not good for your competitive  essay  okay  let's  also now take a quick look at what  readers are out there and available  there's a lot of commercially available  equipment on the market  with  almost all kinds of possible detectors  you can target for an optical readout of  your test you can go for fluorescence  readouts up converting phosphors  magnetic readouts electrical readouts so  really a wide variety from  well-established brands on the market  however  every lattleflow essay system is to some  extent unique  and if you really want to optimize and  remove your uncertainty  you should consider talking to your  reader supplier  to customize your readers  you can  customize it for your test system  but  this can be  that you need to work at different  wavelengths that you  need to install a second camera for  detect